<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Zimbabwe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> is a ruined city of monumental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dry-stone-construction">dry-stone</a> enclosures on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwean plateau</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the centre of a major state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Its decline was followed by the rise of successor states on the plateau. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a> emerged in its wake, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a> later revived its stone-building tradition, making Great Zimbabwe a key reference point for both.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe.jpg" alt="Great Zimbabwe" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Moore from Johannesburg, South Africa · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_(33736918448).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
